// Detection: Registry Run Key / Startup Folder Persistence
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1547.001 (Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder)
// Log source: Microsoft Defender XDR — DeviceRegistryEvents
//
// What it detects:
//   New or modified values under the classic Run/RunOnce autostart
//   registry keys, written by a process that is not a known installer
//   or IT management tool.
//
// Why it matters:
//   Autorun registry keys are one of the oldest and still most common
//   persistence mechanisms because they're simple, reliable across
//   reboots, and easy to miss in manual review. Alerting on writes to
//   these keys from unexpected processes catches malware and
//   post-exploitation implants early.
//
// False positive considerations:
//   - Legitimate software installers (Adobe, browsers, Teams, printer
//     drivers) routinely write to Run keys — allowlist known installer
//     paths/publishers rather than the key itself.
//   - Group Policy or Intune-pushed configuration changes can appear as
//     bulk writes across many devices at once; correlate with your
//     change management calendar before treating as anomalous.

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(1d)
| where ActionType in ("RegistryValueSet", "RegistryKeyCreated")
| where RegistryKey has_any (
    @"Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run",
    @"Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce"
)
| where InitiatingProcessFileName !in~ ("msiexec.exe", "trustedinstaller.exe", "explorer.exe")
| project Timestamp, DeviceName, RegistryKey, RegistryValueName, RegistryValueData,
    InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessAccountName
| order by Timestamp desc
